cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "MAG-S3: what a recording experiment needs to see the shape of the quantum compass"
type: software
version: "0.1.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24043500"
repository-code: "https://github.com/nikabri88-cmd/quantum-compass-recording"
authors:
  - family-names: Ryzhkova
    given-names: Julia
    affiliation: Siriona
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-4194-6059"
date-released: 2026-11-07
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Simulation study of the recording experiment that could test whether neurons reading a radical-pair (cryptochrome)
  magnetic compass carry a population code shaped like the real projective plane RP2, while a polarity-sensitive
  control gives a sphere. The model adds a realistic yield pattern, a modulation depth of the firing rate set directly,
  Poisson spikes, drift, shared gain noise, slow field rotations, simultaneous recordings and pseudo-populations from
  probe and tetrode sessions, and a share of tuned cells with selection. The protocol, code and thresholds were frozen
  before the confirmatory run and their SHA-256 posted before it. In the confirmatory run the realistic pattern kept
  RP2 in 82 of 100 virtual birds, the polar control gave a sphere in 100 of 100, no control readout got a false RP2,
  and an antipodal test of parity needed half the cells topology needed and saw an odd admixture 20 times earlier.
  The hypothesis that pseudo-populations suffer less from shared gain noise failed in the opposite direction; an
  exploratory check traces this to the gain noise of each session and a normalisation by the summed count of the
  session that restores the shape. Code is released under MIT; text, the protocol and the results under CC BY 4.0.
keywords:
  - magnetoreception
  - radical pair
  - cryptochrome
  - electrophysiology
  - experimental design
  - persistent homology
  - topological data analysis
  - neural manifold
  - projective plane
  - population coding
  - simulation
